content":[{"type":"text","text":"Repeat each line out loud, copying the speed and melody of the speaker."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Write down the numbers, times and prices you hear — they appear in listening tasks all the time."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Week 7: Reading and writing practice"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read the short "},{"type":"text","text":"A1 reading texts","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/lessons/a1/reading","target":null,"rel":"noopener noreferrer"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" and answer simple questions about them. Then write your own short texts of 5–8 sentences:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Introduce yourself: name, country, city, job, family."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Describe your normal working day."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Write a short message to a colleague: you are sick and cannot come to work today."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Keep sentences short and correct rather than long and risky. At A1, clear and simple wins."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Week 8: Speaking and a full practice run"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Prepare short spoken answers about the topics examiners usually ask about: yourself, your family, your work, your home, your free time and your plans. Record yourself on your phone, listen back and fix what sounds unclear."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In the last few days, do a full practice run of all three parts in one sitting so the real exam feels familiar. Registered users can take timed practice tests in "},{"type":"text","text":"Exam Prep","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/exam-prep","target":null,"rel":"noopener noreferrer"}}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tips for exam day"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Bring your identity document and arrive 30 minutes early."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In the speaking part, it is fine to say "},{"type":"text","text":"Atsiprašau, nesupratau. Gal galite pakartoti?","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (Sorry, I didn''t understand. Could you repeat?)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Answer in full, simple sentences: "},{"type":"text","text":"Aš gyvenu Vilniuje. Aš dirbu parduotuvėje.","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Do not leave writing tasks empty — a short, correct answer is better than nothing."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ready to start? Begin with week one: the "},{"type":"text","text":"Lithuanian alphabet and pronunciation","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/lessons/a1/fundamentals","target":null,"rel":"noopener noreferrer"}}]},{"type":"text","text":". "},{"type":"text","text":"Sėkmės!","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — Good luck!"}]}]}'::jsonb,
       '<p>The Lithuanian A1 exam tests the basics: can you read and write simple texts, understand slow everyday speech, and hold a short conversation? You do not need perfect grammar — you need solid everyday vocabulary, clear pronunciation and the confidence to answer simple questions.</p>
<p>This plan assumes you start from zero and can study <strong>30–45 minutes a day</strong>. If you have more time, move faster. For who needs the exam, the fee and how to register, read our <a href="/lithuanian-a1-exam" rel="noopener noreferrer">Lithuanian A1 exam guide</a> first.</p>
<h2 id="what-the-a1-exam-checks">What the A1 exam checks</h2>
<p>The exam has three parts. Knowing them helps you practise the right skills from the start:</p>
<ul><li><p><strong>Reading and writing</strong> — short texts such as notices, messages and forms, and writing simple sentences about yourself.</p></li><li><p><strong>Listening</strong> — understanding short, slow everyday speech: prices, times, directions, simple questions.</p></li><li><p><strong>Speaking</strong> — a short conversation with an examiner about familiar topics like your family, work and daily routine.</p></li></ul>
<h2 id="weeks-12-alphabet-sounds-and-survival-words">Weeks 1–2: Alphabet, sounds and survival words</h2>
<p>Lithuanian is spelled almost exactly as it sounds, so learning the letters properly pays off for the whole course. Start with the <a href="/lessons/a1/fundamentals" rel="noopener noreferrer">alphabet and pronunciation lesson</a> and listen to every special letter: ą, č, ę, ė, į, š, ų, ū, ž.</p>
<ul><li><p>Learn greetings and polite words: <strong>labas</strong>, <strong>ačiū</strong>, <strong>prašom</strong>, <strong>atsiprašau</strong>, <strong>viso gero</strong>.</p></li><li><p>Learn numbers 1–100 — you will need them for prices, times and phone numbers.</p></li><li><p>Learn the days of the week and the months.</p></li><li><p>Spend 10 minutes a day on <a href="/flashcards" rel="noopener noreferrer">flashcards</a> and say every word out loud.</p></li></ul>
<h2 id="weeks-34-core-grammar-and-everyday-vocabulary">Weeks 3–4: Core grammar and everyday vocabulary</h2>
<p>Now add the grammar that holds simple sentences together. Work through the <a href="/lessons/a1/grammar" rel="noopener noreferrer">A1 grammar lessons</a> in this order:</p>
<ol><li><p>The verb <strong>būti</strong> (to be): aš esu, tu esi, jis / ji yra.</p></li><li><p>Noun gender: most masculine nouns end in -as, -is, -ys or -us, and most feminine nouns end in -a or -ė.</p></li><li><p>The present tense of regular verbs: aš dirbu, aš gyvenu, aš kalbu.</p></li><li><p>Question words: kas, kur, kada, kodėl, kiek, kaip.</p></li></ol>
<p>At the same time, learn about 15 new words a day from the <a href="/vocabulary" rel="noopener noreferrer">vocabulary list</a>, focusing on family, work, food, shopping and time.</p>
<h2 id="weeks-56-listening-and-real-life-dialogues">Weeks 5–6: Listening and real-life dialogues</h2>
<p>Listening is a skill you only build by listening a lot. Use the <a href="/lessons/a1/dialogues" rel="noopener noreferrer">everyday dialogues</a> — meeting someone, at the shop, at the doctor, asking for directions and at a restaurant.</p>
<ul><li><p>First listen without reading. Then read along. Then listen again without the text.</p></li><li><p>Repeat each line out loud, copying the speed and melody of the speaker.</p></li><li><p>Write down the numbers, times and prices you hear — they appear in listening tasks all the time.</p></li></ul>
<h2 id="week-7-reading-and-writing-practice">Week 7: Reading and writing practice</h2>
<p>Read the short <a href="/lessons/a1/reading" rel="noopener noreferrer">A1 reading texts</a> and answer simple questions about them. Then write your own short texts of 5–8 sentences:</p>
<ul><li><p>Introduce yourself: name, country, city, job, family.</p></li><li><p>Describe your normal working day.</p></li><li><p>Write a short message to a colleague: you are sick and cannot come to work today.</p></li></ul>
<p>Keep sentences short and correct rather than long and risky. At A1, clear and simple wins.</p>
<h2 id="week-8-speaking-and-a-full-practice-run">Week 8: Speaking and a full practice run</h2>
<p>Prepare short spoken answers about the topics examiners usually ask about: yourself, your family, your work, your home, your free time and your plans. Record yourself on your phone, listen back and fix what sounds unclear.</p>
<p>In the last few days, do a full practice run of all three parts in one sitting so the real exam feels familiar. Registered users can take timed practice tests in <a href="/exam-prep" rel="noopener noreferrer">Exam Prep</a>.</p>
<h2 id="tips-for-exam-day">Tips for exam day</h2>
<ul><li><p>Bring your identity document and arrive 30 minutes early.</p></li><li><p>In the speaking part, it is fine to say <strong>Atsiprašau, nesupratau. Gal galite pakartoti?</strong> (Sorry, I didn''t understand. Could you repeat?).</p></li><li><p>Answer in full, simple sentences: <strong>Aš gyvenu Vilniuje. Aš dirbu parduotuvėje.</strong></p></li><li><p>Do not leave writing tasks empty — a short, correct answer is better than nothing.</p></li></ul>
<p>Ready to start? Begin with week one: the <a href="/lessons/a1/fundamentals" rel="noopener noreferrer">Lithuanian alphabet and pronunciation</a>. <strong>Sėkmės!</strong> — Good luck!</p>',
       array['The A1 exam has three parts: reading and writing, listening, and a short conversation with an examiner.', 'About 30–45 minutes of practice a day for 8 weeks is a realistic path to A1 for most beginners.', 'Learn pronunciation first — it makes every later word easier to read, hear and say.', 'Practise speaking out loud from week one; the conversation part is where most people feel least ready.', 'All the lessons, vocabulary and flashcards in this plan are free on KalbaLab and need no account.']::text[],
       '[{"id":"what-the-a1-exam-checks","text":"What the A1 exam checks","level":2},{"id":"weeks-12-alphabet-sounds-and-survival-words","text":"Weeks 1–2: Alphabet, sounds and survival words","level":2},{"id":"weeks-34-core-grammar-and-everyday-vocabulary","text":"Weeks 3–4: Core grammar and everyday vocabulary","level":2},{"id":"weeks-56-listening-and-real-life-dialogues","text":"Weeks 5–6: Listening and real-life dialogues","level":2},{"id":"week-7-reading-and-writing-practice","text":"Week 7: Reading and writing practice","level":2},{"id":"week-8-speaking-and-a-full-practice-run","text":"Week 8: Speaking and a full practice run","level":2},{"id":"tips-for-exam-day","text":"Tips for exam day","level":2}]'::jsonb, '[{"question":"How long does it take to reach A1 in Lithuanian?","answer":"Most beginners who study 30–45 minutes a day can reach A1 in about two to three months. It depends on how much time you have and whether you already know a related language."},{"question":"Can I prepare for the Lithuanian A1 exam on my own?","answer":"Yes. Many people prepare alone with free online lessons, vocabulary lists and audio. The hardest part to practise alone is speaking, so record yourself and talk with Lithuanian colleagues or neighbours whenever you can."},{"question":"Where can I find the official exam rules and dates?","answer":"The National Agency for Education (NŠA) publishes the rules, fee and timetable. Our Lithuanian A1 exam guide summarises them in plain language and links to every official source."}]'::jsonb, '{"title":"Lithuanian A1 Exam Preparation: 8-Week Study Plan","description":"A realistic 8-week plan for the Lithuanian A1 exam: what to study each week for reading, writing, listening and speaking — free, with native audio."}'::jsonb
from post;

with author as (
  select id from public.profiles where is_admin order by created_at limit 1
), post as (
  insert into public.blog_posts (slug, status, category_slug, primary_locale, tags, featured_image, author_id, reading_minutes)
  select 'lithuanian-alphabet-pronunciation-guide', 'draft', 'pronunciation', 'en', array['alphabet', 'pronunciation', 'beginners']::text[],
         '{"url":"https://kalbalab.lt/social-share-kalbalab.png","width":1730,"height":909,"alt":"The Lithuanian Alphabet: A Beginner''s Pronunciation Guide to All 32 Letters"}'::jsonb, author.id, 3
  from author
  on conflict (slug) do nothing
  returning id
)
insert into public.blog_post_translations (post_id, locale, title, excerpt, body_json, body_html, key_takeaways, headings, faq, seo)
select post.id, 'en', 'The Lithuanian Alphabet: A Beginner''s Pronunciation Guide to All 32 Letters', 'Lithuanian has 32 letters and is spelled almost exactly as it sounds. Learn how to pronounce every letter — including ą, č, ę, ė, į, š, ų, ū and ž — with simple English comparisons.',
       '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good news first: Lithuanian is spelled almost exactly as it is pronounced. Once you know the 32 letters, you can read any Lithuanian word out loud — even if you do not know what it means yet."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Below is every letter with an approximate English comparison. English sounds are only a guide, so listen to the native audio for each letter in our "},{"type":"text","text":"alphabet and pronunciation lesson","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/lessons/a1/fundamentals","target":null,"rel":"noopener noreferrer"}}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The 32 letters at a glance"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A Ą B C Č D E Ę Ė F G H I Į Y J K L M N O P R S Š T U Ų Ū V Z Ž","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Q, W and X are not part of the alphabet. F and H appear mostly in borrowed words such as "},{"type":"text","text":"fotografas","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (photographer) and "},{"type":"text","text":"humoras","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (humour)."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Vowels"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A a","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — like ''a'' in ''father'', but short: "},{"type":"text","text":"namas","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (house)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ą ą","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — a long ''a'': "},{"type":"text","text":"ąsotis","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (jug)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"E e","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — an open sound, close to ''a'' in ''cat'': "},{"type":"text","text":"medus","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (honey)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ę ę","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — the same open sound, but long: "},{"type":"text","text":"tęsti","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (to continue)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ė ė","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — a long, closed ''e'', a bit like ''ay'' in ''day'' without the glide: "},{"type":"text","text":"gėlė","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (flower)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I i","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — short, like ''i'' in ''sit'': "},{"type":"text","text":"ir","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (and)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Į į","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","text":"Y y","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — both a long ''ee'' as in ''see'': "},{"type":"text","text":"įėjimas","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (entrance), "},{"type":"text","text":"tylus","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (quiet)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"O o","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — usually a long ''o'', rounder than in English: "},{"type":"text","text":"sodas","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (garden)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"U u","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — short, like ''u'' in ''put'': "},{"type":"text","text":"upė","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (river)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ų ų","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","text":"Ū ū","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — both a long ''oo'' as in ''moon'': "},{"type":"text","text":"ačiū","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (thank you)."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The ''tail'' letters "},{"type":"text","text":"ą, ę, į, ų","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" were once nasal vowels. In modern Lithuanian they are simply pronounced long — you do not need to say them through your nose."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Consonants that differ from English"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C c","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — ''ts'' as in ''cats'': "},{"type":"text","text":"cukrus","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (sugar)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Č č","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — ''ch'' as in ''church'': "},{"type":"text","text":"čia","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (here)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"J j","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — ''y'' as in ''yes'': "},{"type":"text","text":"jūra","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (sea)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"R r","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — a rolled or tapped r, as in Spanish or Italian: "},{"type":"text","text":"rytas","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (morning)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Š š","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — ''sh'' as in ''shop'': "},{"type":"text","text":"šuo","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (dog)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ž ž","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — like the ''s'' in ''measure'': "},{"type":"text","text":"žuvis","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (fish)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"G g","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — always hard, as in ''go'', never as in ''gym'': "},{"type":"text","text":"gatvė","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (street)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"H h","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — a soft ''h'' as in ''hat'', mostly in loanwords."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The other consonants — "},{"type":"text","text":"b, d, f, k, l, m, n, p, s, t, v, z","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — sound much like their English equivalents. Many consonants become ''soft'' before "},{"type":"text","text":"i","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","text":"e","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":", which is why "},{"type":"text","text":"i","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" after a consonant often only softens it, as in "},{"type":"text","text":"ačiū","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" or "},{"type":"text","text":"čia","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Letter combinations to know"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"ch","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — like ''ch'' in Scottish ''loch'' or German ''Bach'': "},{"type":"text","text":"chemija","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (chemistry)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"dž","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — ''j'' as in ''jam'': "},{"type":"text","text":"džiaugsmas","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (joy)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"ie","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — like ''ye'' in ''yeah'': "},{"type":"text","text":"pienas","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (milk)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"uo","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — like ''wo'' in ''wonder'': "},{"type":"text","text":"duona","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (bread)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"ai","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — like ''i'' in ''kite'': "},{"type":"text","text":"kaip","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (how)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"ei","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" — like ''ay'' in ''say'': "},{"type":"text","text":"eime","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (let''s go)."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What about word stress?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stress is the hardest part of Lithuanian pronunciation for beginners. It can fall on any syllable, it can move when a word changes form, and it is "},{"type":"text","text":"not marked","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" in everyday writing. The best way to learn it is to listen and repeat — which is why every word in our "},{"type":"text","text":"vocabulary list","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/vocabulary","target":null,"rel":"noopener noreferrer"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","text":"flashcards","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/flashcards","target":null,"rel":"noopener noreferrer"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" has native audio."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Practise in 10 minutes a day"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Listen to each special letter in the "},{"type":"text","text":"pronunciation lesson","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/lessons/a1/fundamentals","target":null,"rel":"noopener noreferrer"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" and repeat it out loud."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 10 words from the "},{"type":"text","text":"vocabulary list","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/vocabulary","target":null,"rel":"noopener noreferrer"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" before playing the audio, then check yourself."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Practise tricky pairs: "},{"type":"text","text":"c / č","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" ("},{"type":"text","text":"cukrus / čia","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"), "},{"type":"text","text":"š / ž","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" ("},{"type":"text","text":"šuo / žuvis","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"), "},{"type":"text","text":"e / ė","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":" ("},{"type":"text","text":"medis / mėnuo","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":")."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read one short "},{"type":"text","text":"dialogue","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/lessons/a1/dialogues","target":null,"rel":"noopener noreferrer"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" out loud, line by line, after the speaker."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Once the letters feel natural, you are ready for your first real words. Start with our guide to "},{"type":"text","text":"essential Lithuanian phrases for everyday life","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/blog/essential-lithuanian-phrases-for-everyday-life","target":null,"rel":"noopener noreferrer"}}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]}'::jsonb,
       '<p>Good news first: Lithuanian is spelled almost exactly as it is pronounced. Once you know the 32 letters, you can read any Lithuanian word out loud — even if you do not know what it means yet.</p>
<p>Below is every letter with an approximate English comparison. English sounds are only a guide, so listen to the native audio for each letter in our <a href="/lessons/a1/fundamentals" rel="noopener noreferrer">alphabet and pronunciation lesson</a>.</p>
<h2 id="the-32-letters-at-a-glance">The 32 letters at a glance</h2>
<p><strong>A Ą B C Č D E Ę Ė F G H I Į Y J K L M N O P R S Š T U Ų Ū V Z Ž</strong></p>
<p>Q, W and X are not part of the alphabet. F and H appear mostly in borrowed words such as <strong>fotografas</strong> (photographer) and <strong>humoras</strong> (humour).</p>
<h2 id="vowels">Vowels</h2>
<ul><li><p><strong>A a</strong> — like ''a'' in ''father'', but short: <strong>namas</strong> (house).</p></li><li><p><strong>Ą ą</strong> — a long ''a'': <strong>ąsotis</strong> (jug).</p></li><li><p><strong>E e</strong> — an open sound, close to ''a'' in ''cat'': <strong>medus</strong> (honey).</p></li><li><p><strong>Ę ę</strong> — the same open sound, but long: <strong>tęsti</strong> (to continue).</p></li><li><p><strong>Ė ė</strong> — a long, closed ''e'', a bit like ''ay'' in ''day'' without the glide: <strong>gėlė</strong> (flower).</p></li><li><p><strong>I i</strong> — short, like ''i'' in ''sit'': <strong>ir</strong> (and).</p></li><li><p><strong>Į į</strong> and <strong>Y y</strong> — both a long ''ee'' as in ''see'': <strong>įėjimas</strong> (entrance), <strong>tylus</strong> (quiet).</p></li><li><p><strong>O o</strong> — usually a long ''o'', rounder than in English: <strong>sodas</strong> (garden).</p></li><li><p><strong>U u</strong> — short, like ''u'' in ''put'': <strong>upė</strong> (river).</p></li><li><p><strong>Ų ų</strong> and <strong>Ū ū</strong> — both a long ''oo'' as in ''moon'': <strong>ačiū</strong> (thank you).</p></li></ul>
<p>The ''tail'' letters <strong>ą, ę, į, ų</strong> were once nasal vowels. In modern Lithuanian they are simply pronounced long — you do not need to say them through your nose.</p>
<h2 id="consonants-that-differ-from-english">Consonants that differ from English</h2>
<ul><li><p><strong>C c</strong> — ''ts'' as in ''cats'': <strong>cukrus</strong> (sugar).</p></li><li><p><strong>Č č</strong> — ''ch'' as in ''church'': <strong>čia</strong> (here).</p></li><li><p><strong>J j</strong> — ''y'' as in ''yes'': <strong>jūra</strong> (sea).</p></li><li><p><strong>R r</strong> — a rolled or tapped r, as in Spanish or Italian: <strong>rytas</strong> (morning).</p></li><li><p><strong>Š š</strong> — ''sh'' as in ''shop'': <strong>šuo</strong> (dog).</p></li><li><p><strong>Ž ž</strong> — like the ''s'' in ''measure'': <strong>žuvis</strong> (fish).</p></li><li><p><strong>G g</strong> — always hard, as in ''go'', never as in ''gym'': <strong>gatvė</strong> (street).</p></li><li><p><strong>H h</strong> — a soft ''h'' as in ''hat'', mostly in loanwords.</p></li></ul>
<p>The other consonants — <strong>b, d, f, k, l, m, n, p, s, t, v, z</strong> — sound much like their English equivalents. Many consonants become ''soft'' before <strong>i</strong> and <strong>e</strong>, which is why <strong>i</strong> after a consonant often only softens it, as in <strong>ačiū</strong> or <strong>čia</strong>.</p>
<h2 id="letter-combinations-to-know">Letter combinations to know</h2>
<ul><li><p><strong>ch</strong> — like ''ch'' in Scottish ''loch'' or German ''Bach'': <strong>chemija</strong> (chemistry).</p></li><li><p><strong>dž</strong> — ''j'' as in ''jam'': <strong>džiaugsmas</strong> (joy).</p></li><li><p><strong>ie</strong> — like ''ye'' in ''yeah'': <strong>pienas</strong> (milk).</p></li><li><p><strong>uo</strong> — like ''wo'' in ''wonder'': <strong>duona</strong> (bread).</p></li><li><p><strong>ai</strong> — like ''i'' in ''kite'': <strong>kaip</strong> (how).</p></li><li><p><strong>ei</strong> — like ''ay'' in ''say'': <strong>eime</strong> (let''s go).</p></li></ul>
<h2 id="what-about-word-stress">What about word stress?</h2>
<p>Stress is the hardest part of Lithuanian pronunciation for beginners. It can fall on any syllable, it can move when a word changes form, and it is <strong>not marked</strong> in everyday writing. The best way to learn it is to listen and repeat — which is why every word in our <a href="/vocabulary" rel="noopener noreferrer">vocabulary list</a> and <a href="/flashcards" rel="noopener noreferrer">flashcards</a> has native audio.</p>
<h2 id="practise-in-10-minutes-a-day">Practise in 10 minutes a day</h2>
<ol><li><p>Listen to each special letter in the <a href="/lessons/a1/fundamentals" rel="noopener noreferrer">pronunciation lesson</a> and repeat it out loud.</p></li><li><p>Read 10 words from the <a href="/vocabulary" rel="noopener noreferrer">vocabulary list</a> before playing the audio, then check yourself.</p></li><li><p>Practise tricky pairs: <strong>c / č</strong> (<strong>cukrus / čia</strong>), <strong>š / ž</strong> (<strong>šuo / žuvis</strong>), <strong>e / ė</strong> (<strong>medis / mėnuo</strong>).</p></li><li><p>Read one short <a href="/lessons/a1/dialogues" rel="noopener noreferrer">dialogue</a> out loud, line by line, after the speaker.</p></li></ol>
<p>Once the letters feel natural, you are ready for your first real words. Start with our guide to <a href="/blog/essential-lithuanian-phrases-for-everyday-life" rel="noopener noreferrer">essential Lithuanian phrases for everyday life</a>.</p>',
       array['The Lithuanian alphabet has 32 letters, and words are read almost exactly as they are written.', 'Č, š and ž sound like the English ''ch'', ''sh'' and the ''s'' in ''measure''.', 'The letters ą, ę, į and ų are pronounced as long vowels today, not as nasal sounds.', 'Y and į sound the same — a long ''ee'' as in ''see''.', 'Q, W and X are not part of the Lithuanian alphabet.']::text[],
       '[{"id":"the-32-letters-at-a-glance","text":"The 32 letters at a glance","level":2},{"id":"vowels","text":"Vowels","level":2},{"id":"consonants-that-differ-from-english","text":"Consonants that differ from English","level":2},{"id":"letter-combinations-to-know","text":"Letter combinations to know","level":2},{"id":"what-about-word-stress","text":"What about word stress?","level":2},{"id":"practise-in-10-minutes-a-day","text":"Practise in 10 minutes a day","level":2}]'::jsonb, '[{"question":"How many letters are in the Lithuanian alphabet?","answer":"32. It uses the Latin alphabet without Q, W and X, and adds nine letters with diacritics: ą, č, ę, ė, į, š, ų, ū and ž."},{"question":"Is Lithuanian pronunciation difficult?","answer":"Reading is easy because spelling is very regular. The harder parts are the rolled r, the difference between short and long vowels, and word stress, which can fall on different syllables and is not marked in normal writing."},{"question":"How do you pronounce ''ačiū''?","answer":"Say ''AH-chyoo''. The č sounds like the ''ch'' in ''church'', and ū is a long ''oo'' sound."}]'::jsonb, '{"title":"Lithuanian Alphabet & Pronunciation: All 32 Letters","description":"How to pronounce all 32 letters of the Lithuanian alphabet, including ą, č, ę, ė, į, š, ų, ū and ž — with simple English sound comparisons."}'::jsonb
from post;

with author as (
  select id from public.profiles where is_admin order by created_at limit 1
), post as (
  insert into public.blog_posts (slug, status, category_slug, primary_locale, tags, featured_image, author_id, reading_minutes)
  select 'essential-lithuanian-phrases-for-everyday-life', 'draft', 'vocabulary', 'en', array['phrases', 'vocabulary', 'everyday Lithuanian']::text[],
         '{"url":"https://kalbalab.lt/social-share-kalbalab.png","width":1730,"height":909,"alt":"30 Essential Lithuanian Phrases for Everyday Life in Lithuania"}'::jsonb, author.id, 2
  from author
  on conflict (slug) do nothing
  returning id
)
