ry morning."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"These phrases also cover a lot of what you need for the speaking and listening parts of the A1 exam. To plan the rest, see our "},{"type":"text","text":"8-week Lithuanian A1 exam study plan","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"/blog/lithuanian-a1-exam-preparation-plan","target":null,"rel":"noopener noreferrer"}}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]}'::jsonb,
       '<p>You do not need fluent Lithuanian to make daily life easier — and nicer. A few well-chosen phrases go a long way in shops, cafés, at work and with your neighbours. People notice and appreciate the effort.</p>
<p>Here are 30 phrases grouped by situation. You can hear every key word with native audio in our free <a href="/vocabulary" rel="noopener noreferrer">vocabulary list</a> and practise them with <a href="/flashcards" rel="noopener noreferrer">flashcards</a>. New to the letters? Read our <a href="/blog/lithuanian-alphabet-pronunciation-guide" rel="noopener noreferrer">Lithuanian alphabet and pronunciation guide</a> first.</p>
<h2 id="greetings-and-goodbyes">Greetings and goodbyes</h2>
<ol><li><p><strong>Labas!</strong> — Hello! (everyday, informal)</p></li><li><p><strong>Sveiki!</strong> — Hello! (polite, or to several people)</p></li><li><p><strong>Laba diena!</strong> — Good day! / Good afternoon!</p></li><li><p><strong>Labas rytas!</strong> — Good morning!</p></li><li><p><strong>Labas vakaras!</strong> — Good evening!</p></li><li><p><strong>Viso gero!</strong> — Goodbye!</p></li><li><p><strong>Iki!</strong> — Bye! / See you!</p></li></ol>
<h2 id="polite-words-you-will-use-every-day">Polite words you will use every day</h2>
<ol><li><p><strong>Ačiū.</strong> / <strong>Labai ačiū.</strong> — Thank you. / Thank you very much.</p></li><li><p><strong>Prašom.</strong> — Please. / You''re welcome.</p></li><li><p><strong>Atsiprašau.</strong> — Excuse me. / Sorry.</p></li><li><p><strong>Taip.</strong> / <strong>Ne.</strong> — Yes. / No.</p></li><li><p><strong>Kaip sekasi?</strong> — How are you? (literally ''How is it going?'')</p></li><li><p><strong>Gerai, ačiū.</strong> — Fine, thank you.</p></li></ol>
<h2 id="introducing-yourself">Introducing yourself</h2>
<ol><li><p><strong>Mano vardas…</strong> — My name is…</p></li><li><p><strong>Koks jūsų vardas?</strong> — What is your name? (polite)</p></li><li><p><strong>Aš esu iš Bangladešo / Indijos / Uzbekistano.</strong> — I am from Bangladesh / India / Uzbekistan.</p></li><li><p><strong>Malonu susipažinti.</strong> — Nice to meet you.</p></li></ol>
<h2 id="when-you-dont-understand">When you don''t understand</h2>
<ol><li><p><strong>Aš nesuprantu.</strong> — I don''t understand.</p></li><li><p><strong>Gal galite pakartoti?</strong> — Could you repeat that?</p></li><li><p><strong>Prašau kalbėti lėčiau.</strong> — Please speak more slowly.</p></li><li><p><strong>Ar kalbate angliškai?</strong> — Do you speak English?</p></li></ol>
<h2 id="shopping-and-paying">Shopping and paying</h2>
<ol><li><p><strong>Kiek kainuoja?</strong> — How much is it?</p></li><li><p><strong>Ar galima mokėti kortele?</strong> — Can I pay by card?</p></li><li><p><strong>Maišelio nereikia, ačiū.</strong> — I don''t need a bag, thank you.</p></li></ol>
<h2 id="asking-for-directions">Asking for directions</h2>
<ol><li><p><strong>Kur yra…?</strong> — Where is…? For example, <strong>Kur yra stotis?</strong> — Where is the station?</p></li><li><p><strong>Į kairę / į dešinę / tiesiai.</strong> — To the left / to the right / straight ahead.</p></li></ol>
<h2 id="at-a-cafe-or-restaurant">At a café or restaurant</h2>
<ol><li><p><strong>Vieną kavą, prašau.</strong> — One coffee, please.</p></li><li><p><strong>Sąskaitą, prašau.</strong> — The bill, please.</p></li></ol>
<h2 id="if-you-need-help">If you need help</h2>
<ol><li><p><strong>Padėkite!</strong> — Help!</p></li><li><p><strong>Aš sergu. Man reikia gydytojo.</strong> — I''m sick. I need a doctor.</p></li></ol>
<p>In an emergency in Lithuania, call <strong>112</strong>.</p>
<h2 id="tu-or-jus-a-quick-note-on-politeness">Tu or jūs? A quick note on politeness</h2>
<p>Lithuanian has two words for ''you''. <strong>Tu</strong> is for friends, family and children. <strong>Jūs</strong> is for everyone else — people you don''t know, older people, shop staff, officials and doctors. If you are not sure, use <strong>jūs</strong>: nobody will mind.</p>
<h2 id="how-to-make-these-phrases-stick">How to make these phrases stick</h2>
<ul><li><p>Learn five phrases a day and use at least one of them in real life the same day.</p></li><li><p>Listen first, then repeat out loud — copy the rhythm, not just the words.</p></li><li><p>Practise them inside real conversations in our <a href="/lessons/a1/dialogues" rel="noopener noreferrer">everyday dialogues</a>.</p></li><li><p>Review with <a href="/flashcards" rel="noopener noreferrer">flashcards</a> for a few minutes every morning.</p></li></ul>
<p>These phrases also cover a lot of what you need for the speaking and listening parts of the A1 exam. To plan the rest, see our <a href="/blog/lithuanian-a1-exam-preparation-plan" rel="noopener noreferrer">8-week Lithuanian A1 exam study plan</a>.</p>',
       array['Labas is the everyday ''hello''; use Laba diena or Sveiki to be more polite.', 'Ačiū (thank you), Prašom (please / you''re welcome) and Atsiprašau (sorry / excuse me) cover most polite situations.', 'Use the polite ''jūs'' form with strangers, shop staff and older people.', 'Kiek kainuoja? (How much is it?) and Kur yra…? (Where is…?) are two of the most useful questions you can learn.', 'Learning phrases with audio helps with both pronunciation and the A1 listening exam.']::text[],
       '[{"id":"greetings-and-goodbyes","text":"Greetings and goodbyes","level":2},{"id":"polite-words-you-will-use-every-day","text":"Polite words you will use every day","level":2},{"id":"introducing-yourself","text":"Introducing yourself","level":2},{"id":"when-you-dont-understand","text":"When you don''t understand","level":2},{"id":"shopping-and-paying","text":"Shopping and paying","level":2},{"id":"asking-for-directions","text":"Asking for directions","level":2},{"id":"at-a-cafe-or-restaurant","text":"At a café or restaurant","level":2},{"id":"if-you-need-help","text":"If you need help","level":2},{"id":"tu-or-jus-a-quick-note-on-politeness","text":"Tu or jūs? A quick note on politeness","level":2},{"id":"how-to-make-these-phrases-stick","text":"How to make these phrases stick","level":2}]'::jsonb, '[{"question":"How do you say hello in Lithuanian?","answer":"Labas is the everyday, informal ''hello''. Laba diena (good day) and Sveiki are more polite and work with anyone."},{"question":"How do you say thank you in Lithuanian?","answer":"Ačiū (pronounced ''AH-chyoo''). To say ''thank you very much'', say Labai ačiū."},{"question":"What is the polite form of ''you'' in Lithuanian?","answer":"Jūs. Use it with people you don''t know well, older people and in shops, offices and at the doctor. Tu is for friends, family and children."}]'::jsonb, '{"title":"30 Essential Lithuanian Phrases for Everyday Life","description":"The Lithuanian phrases you''ll use every day: greetings, polite words, shopping, directions, cafés and emergencies — with English meanings."}'::jsonb
